A PRODUCT SOURCE THAT REACHES THE HOST PLATFORM'S UNICODE NORMALIZER, so rule N23's rejecting
direction is decided by an input rather than by a comment. It is written the way the regression
would arrive: `normalize` refuses non-ASCII input until slice U3 lands, and the nearest tool that
makes the refusal go away is one call on the string.

It uses all three identifiers the rule knows - the call, the query and the form enumeration - and
it also NAMES `string.Normalize()` in a comment and in a string literal, which the rule must not
report: it reads identifier tokens, so prose that explains the prohibition is not a violation of
it.

```csharp
using System.Text;

namespace Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript;

internal sealed partial class JsRealm
{
    // Not string.Normalize(NormalizationForm.FormC): that is what the rule is about.
    private const string Explanation = "string.Normalize() and IsNormalized() are forbidden";

    private static string ToNfc(string text) =>
        text.IsNormalized() ? text : text.Normalize(NormalizationForm.FormC);
}
```

This file is a witness and is never compiled. It lives here rather than in a product project for
the reason every rejecting witness does: a file that would make the rule fail cannot live where the
rule looks.
